    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-28",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "4 12C  Process for amending consents",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-4-28"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.73",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to this rule, if an accredited person allows CDR consumers to amend\nconsents, it must allow them to do so in the same manner that it asks for CDR\nconsumers to give consents.\nExample:\nIf an accredited person asks a CDR consumer who gave a consent as a CDR business\nconsumer to amend a consent of a kind mentioned in paragraph 1.10A(10)(a), the\naccredited person must invite the CDR consumer to provide a further business\nconsumer statement: see paragraph 4.11(1)(bb).\n(3) In the case of an amendment to a consent, in addition to the information referred\nto in subrule 4.11(3), the accredited person must give the CDR consumer:\n(a) a statement that indicates the consequences of amending a consent; and\n(b) a statement that the accredited person will be able to continue to use any\nCDR data that has already been disclosed to it to the extent allowed by the\namended consent.\n(4) If the CDR consumer gave the consent as a CDR business consumer, the\naccredited person must take reasonable steps to re-confirm that:\n(a) the CDR consumer is not an individual; or\n(b) the CDR consumer has an active ABN.\nNote:\nSee subrule 1.10A(9).\nSubdivision 4.3.2B—Withdrawing consents",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Subject to this rule, if an accredited person allows CDR consumers to amend\nconsents, it must allow them to do so in the same manner that it asks for CDR\nconsumers to give consents.\nExample:\nIf an accredited person asks a CDR consumer who gave a consent as a CDR business\nconsumer to amend a consent of a kind mentioned in paragraph 1.10A(10)(a), the\naccredited person must invite the CDR consumer to provide a further business\nconsumer statement: see paragraph 4.11(1)(bb).\n(3) In the case of an amendment to a consent, in addition to the information referred\nto in subrule 4.11(3), the accredited person must give the CDR consumer:\n(a) a statement that indicates the consequences of amending a consent; and\n(b) a statement that the accredited person will be able to continue to use any\nCDR data that has already been disclosed to it to the extent allowed by the\namended consent.\n(4) If the CDR consumer gave the consent as a CDR business consumer, the\naccredited person must take reasonable steps to re-confirm that:\n(a) the CDR consumer is not an individual; or\n(b) the CDR consumer has an active ABN.\nNote:\nSee subrule 1.10A(9).\nSubdivision 4.3.2B—Withdrawing consents",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-28-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-28-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-28-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "0b99461922f0b8ff8a56a33910d94b65120a9fe92ad1365220aea7adc48002a3"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-4-28",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "4 12C  Process for amending consents",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 73,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to this rule, if an accredited person allows CDR consumers to amend\nconsents, it must allow them to do so in the same manner that it asks for CDR\nconsumers to give consents.\nExample:\nIf an accredited person asks a CDR consumer who gave a consent as a CDR business\nconsumer to amend a consent of a kind mentioned in paragraph 1.10A(10)(a), the\naccredited person must invite the CDR consumer to provide a further business\nconsumer statement: see paragraph 4.11(1)(bb).\n(3) In the case of an amendment to a consent, in addition to the information referred\nto in subrule 4.11(3), the accredited person must give the CDR consumer:\n(a) a statement that indicates the consequences of amending a consent; and\n(b) a statement that the accredited person will be able to continue to use any\nCDR data that has already been disclosed to it to the extent allowed by the\namended consent.\n(4) If the CDR consumer gave the consent as a CDR business consumer, the\naccredited person must take reasonable steps to re-confirm that:\n(a) the CDR consumer is not an individual; or\n(b) the CDR consumer has an active ABN.\nNote:\nSee subrule 1.10A(9).\nSubdivision 4.3.2B—Withdrawing consents"
    },
