    {
      "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-29",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "4 13  Withdrawal of consents",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-4-29"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.73",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A CDR consumer who has given a consent to an accredited person for the\npurposes of this Division may withdraw the consent at any time:\n(a) by using the accredited person’s consumer dashboard; or\n(b) by using a simple alternative method of communication to be made\navailable by the accredited person for that purpose.\n(2) If the consent is withdrawn under paragraph (1)(b), the accredited person must\ngive effect to the withdrawal as soon as practicable, and in any case within 2\nbusiness days after receiving the communication.\nNote:\nThis subrule is a civil penalty provision (see rule 9.8).\n(3) Withdrawal of a consent does not affect an election under rule 4.16 that the CDR\nconsumer’s collected CDR data be deleted once it becomes redundant.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A CDR consumer who has given a consent to an accredited person for the\npurposes of this Division may withdraw the consent at any time:\n(a) by using the accredited person’s consumer dashboard; or\n(b) by using a simple alternative method of communication to be made\navailable by the accredited person for that purpose.\n(2) If the consent is withdrawn under paragraph (1)(b), the accredited person must\ngive effect to the withdrawal as soon as practicable, and in any case within 2\nbusiness days after receiving the communication.\nNote:\nThis subrule is a civil penalty provision (see rule 9.8).\n(3) Withdrawal of a consent does not affect an election under rule 4.16 that the CDR\nconsumer’s collected CDR data be deleted once it becomes redundant.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-29-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-29-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-29-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "91985f8cc1597f95a05604cbf31d4ad0699805833cb59712390fb4e1363f2ec6"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-4-29",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "4 13  Withdrawal of consents",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 73,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A CDR consumer who has given a consent to an accredited person for the\npurposes of this Division may withdraw the consent at any time:\n(a) by using the accredited person’s consumer dashboard; or\n(b) by using a simple alternative method of communication to be made\navailable by the accredited person for that purpose.\n(2) If the consent is withdrawn under paragraph (1)(b), the accredited person must\ngive effect to the withdrawal as soon as practicable, and in any case within 2\nbusiness days after receiving the communication.\nNote:\nThis subrule is a civil penalty provision (see rule 9.8).\n(3) Withdrawal of a consent does not affect an election under rule 4.16 that the CDR\nconsumer’s collected CDR data be deleted once it becomes redundant."
    },
