      "cml:premiseText": "(3) This rule does not apply if the accredited person:"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-35",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "4 17  Information relating to redundant data",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-4-35"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.76",
      "values:originalText": "(1) For subparagraph 4.11(3)(h)(i), the accredited person must state whether they\nhave a general policy, when collected CDR data becomes redundant data, of:\n(a) deleting the redundant data; or\n(b) de-identifying the redundant data; or\n(c) deciding, when the CDR data becomes redundant data, whether to delete it\nor de-identify it.\n(2) An accredited person that gives the statement referred to in paragraph (1)(b) or\n(c) must also state:\n(a) that, if it de-identifies the redundant data:\n(i) it would apply the CDR data de-identification process; and\n(ii) it would be able to use or, if applicable, disclose (by sale or otherwise)\nthe de-identified redundant data without seeking further consent from\nthe CDR consumer; and\n(b) what de-identification of CDR data in accordance with the CDR data\nde-identification process means; and\n(c) if applicable, examples of how it could use the redundant data once\nde-identified.\nNote:\nFor the CDR data de-identification process, see rule 1.17.\nSubdivision 4.3.5—Notification requirements",
      "skos:definition": "(1) For subparagraph 4.11(3)(h)(i), the accredited person must state whether they\nhave a general policy, when collected CDR data becomes redundant data, of:\n(a) deleting the redundant data; or\n(b) de-identifying the redundant data; or\n(c) deciding, when the CDR data becomes redundant data, whether to delete it\nor de-identify it.\n(2) An accredited person that gives the statement referred to in paragraph (1)(b) or\n(c) must also state:\n(a) that, if it de-identifies the redundant data:\n(i) it would apply the CDR data de-identification process; and\n(ii) it would be able to use or, if applicable, disclose (by sale or otherwise)\nthe de-identified redundant data without seeking further consent from\nthe CDR consumer; and\n(b) what de-identification of CDR data in accordance with the CDR data\nde-identification process means; and\n(c) if applicable, examples of how it could use the redundant data once\nde-identified.\nNote:\nFor the CDR data de-identification process, see rule 1.17.\nSubdivision 4.3.5—Notification requirements",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-35-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-4-35-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "57e2ab125b7e7c7b1a6b45ed0da6d7d5e6b88f79a437abf194214c4a20154a60"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-4-35",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "4 17  Information relating to redundant data",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 76,
      "values:originalText": "(1) For subparagraph 4.11(3)(h)(i), the accredited person must state whether they\nhave a general policy, when collected CDR data becomes redundant data, of:\n(a) deleting the redundant data; or\n(b) de-identifying the redundant data; or\n(c) deciding, when the CDR data becomes redundant data, whether to delete it\nor de-identify it.\n(2) An accredited person that gives the statement referred to in paragraph (1)(b) or\n(c) must also state:\n(a) that, if it de-identifies the redundant data:\n(i) it would apply the CDR data de-identification process; and\n(ii) it would be able to use or, if applicable, disclose (by sale or otherwise)\nthe de-identified redundant data without seeking further consent from\nthe CDR consumer; and\n(b) what de-identification of CDR data in accordance with the CDR data\nde-identification process means; and\n(c) if applicable, examples of how it could use the redundant data once\nde-identified.\nNote:\nFor the CDR data de-identification process, see rule 1.17.\nSubdivision 4.3.5—Notification requirements"
    },
