    {
      "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-5-40",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "5 34  Temporary direction to refrain from processing consumer data requests",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-5-40"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.122",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Accreditation Registrar may, by written notice:\n(a) direct an accredited person not to make consumer data requests; or\n(b) direct a data holder not to respond to consumer data requests;\nfor a period of up to 10 days, if the Accreditation Registrar reasonably believes it\nis necessary to do so in order to ensure the security, integrity and stability of the\nRegister or associated database.\n(2) The notice must specify:\n(a) whether the direction applies to all consumer data requests or to requests\nmade to a particular data holder or by a particular accredited person; and\n(b) the period of application.\n(3) Before, or as soon as practicable after, giving a direction, the Accreditation\nRegistrar must give the accredited person or data holder a reasonable opportunity\nto be heard in relation to the matter.\n(4) Despite anything else in these rules:\n(a) an accredited person must not make a consumer data request contrary to a\ndirection it has received under this rule; and\n(b) a data holder must not disclose CDR data in response to a consumer data\nrequest contrary to a direction it has received under this rule.\nCivil penalty:\n(a) for an individual―$50,000; and\n(b) for a body corporate―$250,000.\n\n\nRules relating to dispute resolution  Part 6\nRules relating to Register of Accredited Persons  Division 5.3\nSection 6.1\nCompetition and Consumer (Consumer Data Right) Rules 2020",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Accreditation Registrar may, by written notice:\n(a) direct an accredited person not to make consumer data requests; or\n(b) direct a data holder not to respond to consumer data requests;\nfor a period of up to 10 days, if the Accreditation Registrar reasonably believes it\nis necessary to do so in order to ensure the security, integrity and stability of the\nRegister or associated database.\n(2) The notice must specify:\n(a) whether the direction applies to all consumer data requests or to requests\nmade to a particular data holder or by a particular accredited person; and\n(b) the period of application.\n(3) Before, or as soon as practicable after, giving a direction, the Accreditation\nRegistrar must give the accredited person or data holder a reasonable opportunity\nto be heard in relation to the matter.\n(4) Despite anything else in these rules:\n(a) an accredited person must not make a consumer data request contrary to a\ndirection it has received under this rule; and\n(b) a data holder must not disclose CDR data in response to a consumer data\nrequest contrary to a direction it has received under this rule.\nCivil penalty:\n(a) for an individual―$50,000; and\n(b) for a body corporate―$250,000.\n\n\nRules relating to dispute resolution  Part 6\nRules relating to Register of Accredited Persons  Division 5.3\nSection 6.1\nCompetition and Consumer (Consumer Data Right) Rules 2020",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-5-40-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-5-40-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-5-40-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-4-sec-5-40-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "df86622d8a485368a1ef12b98ced02bc2e6b96a748f6d4029171ce12062b8fc6"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-4-sec-5-40",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "5 34  Temporary direction to refrain from processing consumer data requests",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 122,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Accreditation Registrar may, by written notice:\n(a) direct an accredited person not to make consumer data requests; or\n(b) direct a data holder not to respond to consumer data requests;\nfor a period of up to 10 days, if the Accreditation Registrar reasonably believes it\nis necessary to do so in order to ensure the security, integrity and stability of the\nRegister or associated database.\n(2) The notice must specify:\n(a) whether the direction applies to all consumer data requests or to requests\nmade to a particular data holder or by a particular accredited person; and\n(b) the period of application.\n(3) Before, or as soon as practicable after, giving a direction, the Accreditation\nRegistrar must give the accredited person or data holder a reasonable opportunity\nto be heard in relation to the matter.\n(4) Despite anything else in these rules:\n(a) an accredited person must not make a consumer data request contrary to a\ndirection it has received under this rule; and\n(b) a data holder must not disclose CDR data in response to a consumer data\nrequest contrary to a direction it has received under this rule.\nCivil penalty:\n(a) for an individual―$50,000; and\n(b) for a body corporate―$250,000.\n\n\nRules relating to dispute resolution  Part 6\nRules relating to Register of Accredited Persons  Division 5.3\nSection 6.1\nCompetition and Consumer (Consumer Data Right) Rules 2020"
    },
    {
