    {
      "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-3-sec-1-11",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "1 4  Meaning of covered product",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-3-sec-1-11"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.181",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A product of a kind specified in column 2 of an item of the table below (however\ndescribed) is a covered product for a sector specified in column 1 of that item if:\n(a) the product is publicly offered by or on behalf of a data holder in that\nsector; and\n(b) the product is offered to customers by way of standard form contracts.\nNote:\nFor paragraph (b), section 27 of the Australian Consumer Law sets out matters that a\ncourt may take into account when determining whether a contract is a standard form\ncontract.\n(2) A product need not be available to all members of the public in order to be\npublicly offered.\nNote:\nA product may be offered subject to eligibility requirements. For instance, it may be\navailable only to a person operating a particular type of business. This does not prevent\nit from being “publicly offered” if it would otherwise fall within the ordinary meaning\nof that term.\nProducts that may be covered products\nItem\nColumn 1: Sector\nColumn 2: Products\nBanking and NBL sectors\n(a) a personal credit or charge card account;\n\nSchedule 3  Provisions relevant to the banking and non-bank lenders sectors",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A product of a kind specified in column 2 of an item of the table below (however\ndescribed) is a covered product for a sector specified in column 1 of that item if:\n(a) the product is publicly offered by or on behalf of a data holder in that\nsector; and\n(b) the product is offered to customers by way of standard form contracts.\nNote:\nFor paragraph (b), section 27 of the Australian Consumer Law sets out matters that a\ncourt may take into account when determining whether a contract is a standard form\ncontract.\n(2) A product need not be available to all members of the public in order to be\npublicly offered.\nNote:\nA product may be offered subject to eligibility requirements. For instance, it may be\navailable only to a person operating a particular type of business. This does not prevent\nit from being “publicly offered” if it would otherwise fall within the ordinary meaning\nof that term.\nProducts that may be covered products\nItem\nColumn 1: Sector\nColumn 2: Products\nBanking and NBL sectors\n(a) a personal credit or charge card account;\n\nSchedule 3  Provisions relevant to the banking and non-bank lenders sectors",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-3-sec-1-11-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-3-sec-1-11-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "1fc32d13fa586da47b924b39e7f14da3cc118fbddddde0968fab16edbb380659"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/made-under-the-competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sch-3-sec-1-11",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "1 4  Meaning of covered product",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 181,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A product of a kind specified in column 2 of an item of the table below (however\ndescribed) is a covered product for a sector specified in column 1 of that item if:\n(a) the product is publicly offered by or on behalf of a data holder in that\nsector; and\n(b) the product is offered to customers by way of standard form contracts.\nNote:\nFor paragraph (b), section 27 of the Australian Consumer Law sets out matters that a\ncourt may take into account when determining whether a contract is a standard form\ncontract.\n(2) A product need not be available to all members of the public in order to be\npublicly offered.\nNote:\nA product may be offered subject to eligibility requirements. For instance, it may be\navailable only to a person operating a particular type of business. This does not prevent\nit from being “publicly offered” if it would otherwise fall within the ordinary meaning\nof that term.\nProducts that may be covered products\nItem\nColumn 1: Sector\nColumn 2: Products\nBanking and NBL sectors\n(a) a personal credit or charge card account;\n\nSchedule 3  Provisions relevant to the banking and non-bank lenders sectors"
    },
